{"word": "kid", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "kid", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/kɪd/", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "kids", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(informal) A child, adolescent, or (loosely) a young adult.", "tags": ["countable", "informal", "uncountable"], "examples": ["She's a kid. It's normal for her to have imaginary friends."], "quotes": [{"text": "“So you’ve got the kid,” said Sikes, when they had all reached the room: closing the door as he spoke. ¶ “Yes, here he is,” replied Nancy. ¶ “Did he come quiet?” inquired Sikes. ¶ “Like a lamb,” rejoined Nancy.", "reference": "1838, Boz [pseudonym; Charles Dickens], “Wherein Oliver Is Delivered over to Mr. William Sikes”, in Oliver Twist; […], volume II, London: Richard Bentley, […], →OCLC, page 12:"}, {"text": "I said, “I’ll send the first sane soul I meet to keep you company.” As luck would have it, I never met one,—only kids, and a baker, who wouldn’t leave his cart, or take it with him either.", "reference": "1897 March 13 – June 19, Richard Marsh [pseudonym; Richard Bernard Heldmann], “What the Tidings Were”, in The Beetle (The Adelphi Library; 4), London: T[homas] Fisher Unwin, […], published 1920, →OCLC, book IV (In Pursuit), page 269:"}, {"text": "\"I should never dream of calling a kid like you a woman,\" said Digory loftily.", "reference": "1955, C[live] S[taples] Lewis, The Magician’s Nephew, London: The Bodley Head, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "‘No,’ said Luke, grinning at her. ‘You’re not dull enough! […] What about the kid’s clothes? I don’t suppose they were anything to write home about, but didn’t you keep anything? A bootee or a bit of embroidery or anything at all?’", "reference": "1963, Margery Allingham, “The Beanspiller”, in The China Governess: A Mystery, London: Chatto & Windus, →OCLC, page 186:"}, {"text": "Our kids are why all of you are in this room today. Our kids are why you wake up wondering how you'll make a difference and go to bed thinking about tomorrow's lesson plan. Our kids are why you walk into that classroom every day even when you're not getting the support, or the pay, or the respect that you deserve - because you believe that every child should have a chance to succeed; that every child can be taught.", "reference": "2007 July 5, Barack Obama, Remarks of Senator Barack Obama to the National Education Association Annual Meeting:"}, {"text": "She not my lover like \"Billie Jean\", but the kid is mine", "reference": "2018, “March 14”, in Scorpion, performed by Drake:"}, {"text": "Network Rail is now the biggest kid in the playground, so if it doesn't want to play it doesn't have to, and the trees still fall down every time someone gives a low pressure system a name.", "reference": "2019 October, Ian Walmsley, “Cleaning up”, in Modern Railways, page 44:"}], "synonyms": ["bairn", "brat", "child", "chit", "crumb cruncher", "crumb crusher", "crumb grinder", "crumb snatcher", "fry", "juvenile", "kid", "kiddie", "little one", "minor", "mini-me", "moppet", "munchkin", "nipper", "slip", "small fry", "sprog", "sprout", "tart", "tot", "tyke", "youngling", "youngster", "young'un", "youth", "crib lizard", "crotch critter", "crotch dropping", "crotch fruit", "crotch goblin", "crotch monkey", "crotchling", "cum cheese", "cum pet", "cunt dropping", "fucklet", "fuck trophy", "hump dumpling", "pet sperm", "semen demon", "shitling", "sperm blossom", "sperm buttercup", "sperm bubbles", "bitchling", "terence"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": [{"definition": "(informal) A person whose childhood took place in a particular time period or area.", "tags": ["countable", "informal", "uncountable"], "examples": ["Only '90s kids will remember this toy.", "He's been living in Los Angeles for years now, but he's a Florida kid."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": ["bairn", "brat", "child", "chit", "crumb cruncher", "crumb